    "scenario": "A 12-year-old girl with polyarticular juvenile rheumatoid arthritis is being treated with ibuprofen and a biologic drug. She is evaluated in the emergency department for dehydration after several days of vomiting and diarrhea related to a viral illness. Initial chemistry results reveal acute kidney injury. She is transferred to the pediatric ICU for monitoring. The attending physician discusses with the team how the fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) could be used to assist in differentiating whether the acute kidney injury is secondary to a prerenal cause or acute tubular necrosis (ATN) from ibuprofen use. Serum and urine chemistry values are: BUN, 60 mg/dL; urine urea, 400 mg/dL; urine sodium, 10 mEq/L; plasma sodium, 148 mEq/L; urine creatinine, 5 mg/dL; and plasma creatinine, 3 mg/dL. Which of the following is the FENa value and what does it indicate?",
